This command is NOT shipped to installing projects. A cold bootstrap skips it, and the
plugin's _hook_binaries manifest does not list it.

seatprobe is a development harness: it shells out to the `claude` CLI, reads the seat
constitutions from this repo's agents/, and writes throwaway run directories. In a consumer's
plugin bin it would be dead weight wearing the same coat as the hook binaries beside it.

It lives under plugins/<name>/tools/cmd/ anyway because Go's internal-package rule leaves it
nowhere else to be: it drives internal/seatprobe, internal/cli and internal/record, and only a
main inside this module may import them.

THE MARKER IS THE FACT, and both readers derive from it rather than keeping their own list:

  scripts/bootstrap-plugins.sh   skips a cmd/ directory holding this file
  scripts/pluginparity           excludes it from the shipped-binary count and the
                                 _hook_binaries cross-check

Two hand-maintained lists is exactly the drift pluginparity exists to catch — it was written
because the documented count went stale twice and the manifest twice more. A third list, kept
by the same hands, would have been the fourth.
